course: books
lesson: 20
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter X, part 2: The Lobster Quadrille (poems gone wrong and Beautiful Soup)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Now the creatures want Alice's adventures. She says she can only tell them from this morning: «it's no use going back to yesterday, because I was a different person then». She starts with the White Rabbit, nervous at first with the two big creatures pressing close on each side, but braver as she goes on.
explain: When she gets to the part where «You are old, Father William» came out all wrong for the Caterpillar, the Mock Turtle wants to hear her try again. The Gryphon orders her to stand up and recite «'Tis the voice of the sluggard». «How the creatures order one about, and make one repeat lessons!» thinks Alice — it's like being at school.
explain: Her head is so full of the Lobster Quadrille that the poem comes out as nonsense about a lobster who has been «baked too brown» and must «sugar his hair». The Mock Turtle calls it «uncommon nonsense» and keeps asking her to explain it. Alice sits with her face in her hands, wondering if anything will ever happen in a natural way again.
explain: The next verse goes wrong too, about an Owl and a Panther sharing a pie, and they let her stop. Alice asks for a song instead, and the Mock Turtle, choking with sobs, sings «Beautiful Soup, so rich and green, Waiting in a hot tureen!» — a sad song about soup, from a creature that soup is made of.
explain: In the middle of the chorus a cry rings out: «The trial's beginning!» The Gryphon grabs Alice's hand and runs, only ever answering «Come on!», while the sad words «Beautiful, beautiful Soup!» fade away behind them on the breeze.
fact: Alice: «I could tell you my adventures—beginning from this morning... but it's no use going back to yesterday, because I was a different person then.»
fact: The Mock Turtle says «Explain all that»; the Gryphon says «No, no! The adventures first: explanations take such a dreadful time.»
fact: The two creatures sit one on each side, very close, with eyes and mouths open «so very wide»; Alice is nervous at first but «gained courage as she went on».
fact: They stay quiet until she reaches «You are old, Father William» coming out different; the Mock Turtle says «That's very curious».
fact: The Gryphon orders «Stand up and repeat "'Tis the voice of the sluggard"» (a sluggard is a lazy person; it was a real poem children learned by heart).
fact: Alice's version: «'Tis the voice of the Lobster; I heard him declare, "You have baked me too brown, I must sugar my hair."»
fact: The Mock Turtle says it «sounds uncommon nonsense» (uncommon = very, in old country talk).
fact: Asked how the Lobster could turn out his toes with his nose, Alice says «It's the first position in dancing» — but she is «dreadfully puzzled by the whole thing».
fact: The next verse begins «I passed by his garden, and marked, with one eye» (marked = noticed), and tells of the Owl and the Panther sharing a pie.
fact: «What is the use of repeating all that stuff, if you don't explain it as you go on?» says the Mock Turtle: «It's by far the most confusing thing I ever heard!», and the Gryphon lets her stop.
fact: The Gryphon asks «Shall we try another figure of the Lobster Quadrille? Or would you like the Mock Turtle to sing you a song?»; when Alice eagerly chooses the song, the Gryphon is offended: «Hm! No accounting for tastes!» (= people like strange things), and asks the Mock Turtle to sing «Turtle Soup».
fact: «Beautiful Soup, so rich and green, Waiting in a hot tureen!» A tureen is a big deep dish for serving soup; «pennyworth» means as much as a penny buys; in the book the line is printed «two p / ennyworth», with the word split across two lines.
fact: «Chorus again!» cries the Gryphon, and the Mock Turtle has just begun it when the cry «The trial's beginning!» is heard in the distance; the Gryphon takes Alice by the hand and runs, answering only «Come on!»
fact: The poems Alice gets wrong in the book are copies of real Victorian children's poems: «How doth the little busy bee», «Father William», «'Tis the voice of the sluggard»; Carroll turns them into nonsense.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «sound» (Liverpool) = fine, good; «boss» (Liverpool) = great; «tamping» (Wales) = furious; «dreich» (Scotland) = grey and gloomy; «slay» = do brilliantly.
exercise: From when does Alice say she can tell her adventures? | From this morning | From the day she was born | From about last week | From yesterday afternoon
exercise: Why does Alice say it's no use going back to yesterday? | She was a different person then | She can't remember yesterday at all | Yesterday was too boring to tell | Her sister told her not to tell anyone
exercise: «Explain all that,» says the Mock Turtle. Why does the Gryphon say «adventures first»? | Explanations take such a dreadful time | Adventures are true and explanations are made up | The Queen wants to hear them first | It is time for soup
exercise: Where does Alice begin her story? | When she first saw the White Rabbit | At the tea-party with the March Hare and the Hatter | At the croquet-ground | In the Duchess's kitchen
exercise: How does Alice feel at the start of telling her story? | A little nervous, with the creatures so close | Very proud and loud, like a queen on a throne, from the first word | Sleepy | Angry
exercise: At what point in Alice's story does the Mock Turtle first speak? | When the words of «Father William» all came different | When Alice fell down the rabbit-hole and landed on a heap of sticks | At the tea-party | At the very end
exercise: What does the Gryphon tell Alice to recite? | «'Tis the voice of the sluggard» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star, how I wonder what you are» | «How doth the little busy bee improve each shining hour» | «Beautiful Soup, so rich and green»
exercise: «I might as well be at school at once.» Why does Alice think this? | The creatures order her about and make her repeat lessons | The Mock Turtle has just told her about his school under the sea, and she misses her own | She wants to learn washing | She sees a schoolroom
exercise: Why does the poem come out wrong this time? | Her head is so full of the Lobster Quadrille | She is very hungry for soup | The Gryphon keeps interrupting her with «Hjckrrh!» after every single line | She has never learnt it
exercise: In Alice's version, what does the Lobster say? | «You have baked me too brown, I must sugar my hair» | «Will you walk a little faster? There's a porpoise close behind us» | «Off with his head» | «I am a real turtle»
exercise: «It sounds uncommon nonsense.» What does «uncommon» mean here? | Very | Rarely heard | Not at all | Strange-looking
exercise: What does Alice do while the Mock Turtle and the Gryphon argue about it? | Sits with her face in her hands | Laughs out loud | Runs away | Sings the soup song
exercise: What does Alice wonder, with her face in her hands? | If anything would ever happen in a natural way again | If the Queen would cut off her head before tea-time or let her go home to Dinah | If the Mock Turtle could swim | If her sister was looking for her
exercise: How could the Lobster turn out his toes with his nose? Alice's answer? | «It's the first position in dancing,» said about the Lobster's toes | «He uses his claws» | «I don't know, sir» | «Lobsters have no toes»
exercise: Which animals share a pie in the next verse? | The Owl and the Panther | The Hatter and the March Hare | The Lobster and the Snail | The Dormouse and the Cat
exercise: Why do they let Alice stop reciting? | The Mock Turtle finds it the most confusing thing he ever heard | She has a very sore throat after all the singing and talking by the sea, and must rest | The trial begins | She forgets the rest
exercise: What does the Gryphon offer Alice next? | Another figure of the Quadrille, or a song from the Mock Turtle | A cup of hot tea with bread-and-butter, like the Hatter's | A game of croquet | A ride on his back
exercise: Alice asks for a song «so eagerly». How does the Gryphon react? | He is offended: «Hm! No accounting for tastes!» | He is delighted and sings too, louder than anyone | He falls asleep | He cries
exercise: What song does the Mock Turtle sing? | «Turtle Soup» — «Beautiful Soup» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» | «Speak roughly to your little boy, and beat him when he sneezes» | «The Lobster Quadrille»
exercise: How does the Mock Turtle sing it? | In a voice sometimes choked with sobs | Loudly and cheerfully, dancing as he sang | In a whisper | In French
exercise: What is a tureen? | A big deep dish for serving soup | A kind of turtle that lives in hot water | A large silver soup spoon with a long handle | A sea-bird that nests on the cliffs
exercise: What colour is the Beautiful Soup? | Green | Bright red | Snow-white | Dark brown
exercise: What cry interrupts the chorus? | «The trial's beginning!» | «The Queen! The Queen is coming this way!» | «Off with his head, at once!» | «Dinner's ready, come and eat!»
exercise: «What trial is it?» asks Alice. What does the Gryphon answer? | Only «Come on!» | «The Knave of Hearts' trial» | «Yours, of course» | «The Hatter's trial»
exercise: What can Alice hear as they run off? | The melancholy words of the soup song, more and more faintly | The Queen shouting «Off with his head!» and stamping her feet on the ground | Lobsters singing | Nothing at all
test: Isla from Edinburgh: «dreich day, and Alice says she was the same person yesterday.» (dreich = grey, gloomy) Put it right. | She said she was a different person yesterday | Right: she said she has been exactly the same since she was born | She said she can't remember | She said yesterday was today
test: «Explanations take such a dreadful time,» says the Gryphon. Who in the book has been asking Alice to explain herself before? | The Caterpillar: «Explain yourself!» | The Cheshire Cat, every time it vanished in front of her | The Duchess | The cook
test: Why does the Mock Turtle find «It all came different!» so interesting? | He wants to hear Alice recite something now and see if it goes wrong | He knows the right words of every poem ever written and wants to correct her, line by line | He wants to teach her washing | He wants to sing
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Alice tells the Lobster poem perfectly.» | False: it comes out as nonsense about a Lobster baked too brown | True: every word was right, and the Gryphon clapped his paws for a long time | True, but the Mock Turtle didn't like it | False: she refuses to say it
test: Josh, tamping (furious): «why's the Mock Turtle so obsessed with explaining stuff?» What does he say? | «What is the use of repeating all that stuff, if you don't explain it as you go on?» | «I want to write it all down for my old teacher, Tortoise, at the sea school» | «Explanations take such a dreadful time» | Nothing; he falls asleep
test: «I must sugar my hair.» What real thing might Alice be mixing up, after «baked me too brown»? | Cooking: sugar on a cake or a pie baked in the oven | Painting the white roses red for the Queen before she comes | Washing with whiting | Croquet with flamingoes
test: Why is it sad, and a bit funny, that the Mock Turtle sings about «Beautiful Soup»? | Mock turtle soup is what he is supposed to be made into | Soup is his favourite food in the whole world and he is very hungry after dancing | He invented soup at school | Soup was taught at his school as an extra
test: Ben from Liverpool: «the Gryphon was boss happy Alice picked the song.» (boss = great) Put it right. | He was offended: «Hm! No accounting for tastes!» | Right: he jumped for joy, waved his paws and sang along at the top of his voice | He didn't care at all | He picked it himself
test: What would happen if Alice told her adventures «going back to yesterday», by her own logic? | They'd be about someone she isn't any more | They'd be the very same adventures as today's, told twice over | She'd need to ask the Queen | She'd fall asleep
test: Why does the Gryphon only answer «Come on!» as they run? | He's in a hurry and, like everyone here, just orders Alice about | He doesn't know any other words at all, except «Hjckrrh!» | He's out of breath from singing | He's angry with Alice
test: Looking back: in chapter II, Alice tried to say «How doth the little busy bee». What came out? | «How doth the little crocodile improve his shining tail» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little bat» | «'Tis the voice of the Lobster; I heard him declare» | «You are old, Father William»
test: Looking back: in chapter V, what did the Caterpillar say about Alice's «Father William»? | «It is wrong from beginning to end» | «That is quite right, well done, child, every word of it» | «I've never heard it before» | «Sing it again»
test: Looking back: in chapter II, Alice wondered «Who in the world am I?» Which line in this part shows she still feels changed? | «It's no use going back to yesterday, because I was a different person then» | «It's the first position in dancing» | «Oh, a song, please» | «What trial is it?»
test: Looking back: in chapter V, Alice told the Caterpillar «I can't explain myself... because I'm not myself». What does this part's line about yesterday have in common with it? | Both say she has changed so much she isn't the same person | Both are about soup | Both are said to the Gryphon | Both are poems
test: Looking back: which other soup has been in the book, and what was wrong with it? | The Duchess's cook's soup, with far too much pepper | The Hatter's soup, with the best butter and crumbs in it | The Queen's soup, made of tarts | The White Rabbit's soup, which was cold
test: Looking back: in chapter IX, the Gryphon had already ordered Alice about. What did Alice think then? | «Everybody says "come on!" here... I never was so ordered about in all my life» | «What a kind creature, to show me the way so nicely» | «I'll go home now» | «I'm a serpent»
test: ★★★ Alice's three wrong poems so far: the crocodile (ch II), Father William (ch V) and the Lobster (ch X). What do the new versions have in common with what was on Alice's mind? | The Lobster one is full of the dance she just saw, so her head mixes in recent things | They are all about cats | They all come from the Queen's own book of poems for children | Nothing at all
test: ★★★ «Who would not give all else for two p / ennyworth only of beautiful Soup?» What is odd about how this line is printed? | The word «pennyworth» is split across two lines | It is printed upside down in the book, so you must turn it round | It has no rhyme | It is in French
test: ★★★ Careful: does the Mock Turtle finish the chorus the second time? | No: the cry «The trial's beginning!» comes just after he begins it | Yes, twice over, with the Gryphon joining in very loudly | Yes, once | He never begins it
exam: Identify the reason adduced by Alice for declining to narrate the events of the preceding day. | That she had then been a different person | That she had no recollection whatsoever of them, owing to her fall | That they were of no interest | That the Gryphon forbade it
exam: It is given that Alice was required to recite a poem. Select the response which most accurately characterises the consequence. | The words came very queer, her head being full of the Lobster Quadrille | She recited it without error and was commended by both auditors | She declined to recite it | She sang it to music
exam: Ascertain the remark of the Gryphon upon Alice's expressed preference for a song. | «Hm! No accounting for tastes!» | «An excellent choice, young lady, for the Mock Turtle sings beautifully» | «Off with her head!» | «Come on!»
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the chapter? | A cry that the trial was beginning caused the Gryphon to hurry away with Alice | The Mock Turtle completed his song twice over, with the Gryphon in chorus | Alice returned to the croquet-ground alone | The Queen arrived to hear the song
